    {
      "title": "Google preinstallation attempt on iPhones",
      "summary": "Pichai admitted in 2018 attempting to get Tim Cook to preinstall Google on iOS devices to connect services, hoping for a win-win where Google gains search users/data and Apple gains revenue from a search agreement, but Cook declined as Apple does not preload third-party software."
    },
    {
      "title": "Google chat log deletion policy",
      "summary": "In February, DOJ accused Google of systematically destroying chats via a history-off option deleting messages every 24 hours; Pichai claimed he took action against the history-off default in February to comply with a litigation hold, though Bellshaw cited a 2021 message exchange where Pichai discussed a personnel matter unrelated to the hold."
    },
    {
      "title": "Internet Explorer default hypocrisy",
      "summary": "In 2005, Microsoft made Internet Explorer the default search engine, prompting Google's legal chief David Drummond to send an angry letter accusing this of anticompetitive behavior; Bellshaw used Drummond's letter to highlight Google's hypocrisy regarding defaults, noting that changes to defaults worsen problems and most users do not change them, arguments also used by DuckDuckGo, Brave, and Bing against Google."
    },
    {
      "title": "Google antitrust case implications",
      "summary": "The case, expected to continue for several weeks, could result in outcomes similar to the 1990s Microsoft trial, requiring Google to change behavior, share APIs, ban exclusive deals with manufacturers, and turn over collected data to competitors; the DOJ notes Google gets 16 times more data than Bing daily, and a ruling may impact ongoing FTC cases against Amazon, Apple, and Facebook."
    }
  ]
}

